 for bestpractices and # Workflow & Automation Engine

## Overview
Develop a comprehensive Workflow & Automation Engine for a multi-tenant, AI-assisted operations platform. This engine provides a visual and programmable environment to define, test, schedule, and execute workflows and automations that react to triggers, evaluate conditional logic, and perform actions across connected integrations and internal OS modules. It must include robust execution tracking (WorkflowRun), persistent workflow definitions (Workflow), an Activity Log for auditing, and a management console to build and operate workflows. All code must enforce strict runtime safety against null/undefined values (as per the runtime safety requirements below). Implement dependency-first ordering: auth/access control and core data setup must be completed before dashboard/workspace/analytics components when both are present.

---

## Components to Build

- [data_structure] WorkflowRun
  - Purpose: Execution records for a workflow run with status, logs, and result metadata.
  - Fields:
    - id: UUID, required
    - workflowId: UUID, required, references Workflow
    - status: string, required (e.g., "Running", "Completed", "Failed", "Cancelled")
    - startedAt: timestamp, optional
    - finishedAt: timestamp, optional
    - logs: JSON, optional (execution logs, per-step metadata, telemetry)
    - resultMetadata: JSON, optional (final results, summaries, metrics)
  - Relationships:
    - Belongs to: Workflow

- [data_structure] Workflow
  - Purpose: Workflow definitions and run history for automations and manual workflows.
  - Fields:
    - id: UUID, required
    - companyId: UUID, required
    - name: string, required
    - definition: JSON, required (structure for triggers, steps, conditions, actions, connectors)
    - status: string, required (e.g., "Active", "Paused", "Draft")
    - createdAt: timestamp, optional
    - updatedAt: timestamp, optional
  - Relationships:
    - Has many: WorkflowRun

- [page] Activity Log
  - Tenant-scoped chronological feed of user actions, workflow runs, integration syncs, and AI actions for auditing and troubleshooting.
  - Features:
    - Filter by tenant, department, user, action type, date range
    - Persisted events: user_action, workflow_run, integration_sync, ai_action, system_alert
    - Pagination and infinite scrolling; ability to export to CSV/JSON
    - Real-time or near-real-time updates via subscription/websocket if supported

- [page] Workflows / Automations
  - Management console for building, testing, scheduling, and running workflows and automations across connected integrations.
  - Features:
    - Create/Edit Workflow: define triggers, conditions, and actions; specify connectors and internal actions
    - Visual Workflow Editor: drag-and-drop nodes for triggers, conditions, actions with wiring and sequencing
    - Test/Preview: simulate runs locally with mock data
    - Scheduling: cron-like or time-based triggers
    - Run History: list of WorkflowRun per workflow with status and logs
    - Access Control: per-tenant, per-role permissions to view/edit/run
    - Integrations Management: link connectors/adapters; handle authentication contexts
    - Audit Trail: per-run decisions, user actions, edits, and approvals
    - Human Approval Steps: insert approval nodes with notification triggers and audit logging
    - Retry/Backoff/Alerts: configure retry strategy and alerting conditions

---

## Implementation Requirements

### Frontend
- Visual Editor
  - Drag-and-drop canvas with a palette of node types: Trigger, Condition, Action, Approval, Delay, Sub-workflow
  - Connections between nodes; validation to prevent cycles or orphaned nodes
  - Real-time validation with inline hints (e.g., missing required fields, invalid data types)
  - Node property panel for editing: display name, configuration fields, connector references
  - Saving: persist workflow definition to backend JSON schema
- Workflows List & Details
  - List view with search, filters (status, owner, last modified), and bulk actions
  - Workflow Detail Page: show definition, run history summary, and quick-test actions
- Run View
  - Live tail of logs for a running WorkflowRun
  - Visual indicators for per-step status (pending, running, success, failed)
- Activity Log Page
  - Timeline-style UI with expandable items; supports filtering and exporting
- Security & Access
  - Role-based UI gating; ensure components respect permissions and tenant isolation
- Accessibility
  - Keyboard navigable editor, proper aria labels, high-contrast friendly modes

Coding guardrails (enforce runtime safety):
- Use data ?? [] for results from API calls when mapping arrays
- Guard all array operations: (items ?? []).map(...) and Array.isArray checks
- Initialize useState with arrays: useState<Workflow[]>([]) and useState<WorkflowRun[]>([])
- Validate API shapes: const list = Array.isArray(response?.data) ? response.data : []
- Optional chaining for nested objects: obj?.prop?.nested
- Destructure with defaults: const { items = [], count = 0 } = response ?? {}

### Backend
- API Endpoints (examples, implement in chosen stack):
  - POST /api/workflows: create a new workflow
  - GET /api/workflows?companyId=&status= - list workflows for a tenant
  - GET /api/workflows/:id: fetch workflow definition
  - PUT /api/workflows/:id: update workflow definition
  - POST /api/workflows/:id/run: trigger a run (on-demand/manual)
  - GET /api/workflow-runs?workflowId=&status=&date range
  - GET /api/activity-log: fetch activity feed with filters
  - POST /api/workflow-runs/:id/logs: append logs or store per-run metadata
  - POST /api/integrations/:integrationId/trigger: external trigger for connectors
- Database Tables (normalized)
  - workflows: id (PK), companyId (FK), name, definition (JSON), status, createdAt, updatedAt
  - workflow_runs: id (PK), workflowId (FK), status, startedAt, finishedAt, logs (JSON), resultMetadata (JSON)
  - activity_logs: id (PK), tenantId, userId, actionType, relatedEntity (workflow_run/workflow/integration), relatedId (FK), timestamp, details (JSON)
  - approvals: id (PK), runId (FK to workflow_runs), approverUserId, decision, notes, timestamp
  - connectors: id (PK), companyId, type, config, status, lastSyncAt
- Data Validation
  - Validate incoming workflow definitions against a formal JSON schema
  - Ensure required fields present; guard against malformed JSON
  - Enforce idempotent execution where supported (e.g., operation id or correlationId)
- Execution Engine (Server-side)
  - Trigger ingestion: receive events from external systems or internal schedulers
  - Evaluate workflow graph: execute nodes in topological order with correct data context
  - Actions: adapters to create/update external records, send messages, internal actions
  - Transactions and idempotency: wrap steps where possible; implement retry/backoff strategies
  - Error handling: catch per-step errors, retry according to policy, escalate with alerts
  - Human approvals: support approval steps with notifications and audit entries
  - Security: enforce tenant-scoped data access; enforce per-user permissions for actions

### Integration
- Connectors/Adapters
  - Implement a pluggable adapter interface for external systems (CRM, messaging, storage, etc.)
  - Support OAuth/API key per-tenant configuration
  - Normalize responses into a unified context model for workflows
- Data Context & Execution Data
  - Maintain a unified data model for context propagation through workflow steps
  - Ensure all actions have access to tenant, user, department, and related records
- Event Bus / Messaging
  - Optional: use an event bus for triggering workflows and for live updates to UI
  - WebSocket/Server-Sent Events for real-time workflow run updates on the frontend

---

## User Experience Flow

1. Admin navigates to Workflows / Automations
2. Create a new workflow:
   - Assign name, select company/tenant scope
   - Define triggers (e.g., on lead created, on KPI threshold, scheduled times)
   - Build the flow with nodes: Condition, Action, Approval, Delay, Sub-workflow
   - Configure each node (e.g., action details, connector targets, conditional expressions)
   - Save draft; validate graph integrity
3. Test & Preview:
   - Run in test mode with sample data to verify logic
   - Inspect per-node outcomes and logs
   - Adjust as needed
4. Schedule or Trigger:
   - Schedule recurring runs or trigger manually
   - Optional: require human approvals for certain steps
5. Run & Monitor:
   - Observe live run in Run View; see per-step status and logs
   - If error occurs, automatic retry/backoff kicks in; if still failing, escalate to alerting and create an audit entry
6. Activity Log:
   - View tenant-scoped activity feed for all actions, workflow runs, integrations, and AI actions
7. Insights & Audit:
   - Access historical runs, logs, and approvals for compliance and troubleshooting
8. Across the platform:
   - Contextualized data feeds populate dashboards, AI workspace, and other modules per tenant permissions

---

## Build Order & Dependencies (Mandatory)

- Prerequisites:
  - Authentication & authorization module with RBAC in place
  - Multi-tenant data isolation and context layer
  - Core unified data model for workflows and runs
  - Database migrations for workflows, workflow_runs, activity_logs, approvals
- Blocks:
  - After core data is available, implement the Workflows/Automations page and the Activity Log page
  - Then implement the Visual Editor and Run/History UIs
  - Finally, add human approvals, scheduling, retries, and alerts
- Sequencing Rule:
  - Auth/access control and core data setup must be completed before dashboard/workspace/analytics pages when both exist

---

## Technical Specifications

- Data Models
  - Workflow
    - id UUID PK
    - companyId UUID FK
    - name string
    - definition JSON (structured triggers, nodes, conditions, actions)
    - status string (Active, Paused, Draft)
    - createdAt timestamp
    - updatedAt timestamp
  - WorkflowRun
    - id UUID PK
    - workflowId UUID FK
    - status string (Running, Completed, Failed)
    - startedAt timestamp
    - finishedAt timestamp
    - logs JSON
    - resultMetadata JSON
  - ActivityLog
    - id UUID PK
    - tenantId UUID
    - userId UUID
    - actionType string
    - relatedEntity string
    - relatedId UUID
    - timestamp timestamp
    - details JSON
  - Approvals
    - id UUID PK
    - runId UUID FK (WorkflowRun)
    - approverUserId UUID
    - decision string
    - notes string
    - timestamp timestamp
- API Endpoints
  - GET /api/workflows?companyId=&status=
  - POST /api/workflows
  - GET /api/workflows/:id
  - PUT /api/workflows/:id
  - POST /api/workflows/:id/run
  - GET /api/workflow-runs?workflowId=&status=&date
  - GET /api/activity-log
  - POST /api/workflow-runs/:id/logs
  - POST /api/approvals/:runId
- Security
  - RBAC and tenant scoping for all endpoints
  - Validate ownership before performing updates
  - Encrypt sensitive connectors/secrets at rest; audit log all sensitive actions
- Validation
  - JSON schema validation for workflow.definition
  - Validate IDs exist and belong to the tenant
  - Guard against null/undefined before accessing array properties

---

## Acceptance Criteria

- [ ] All API responses use null-safe patterns: avoid null/undefined access when mapping arrays; data ?? [] used where applicable
- [ ] Workflow editor saves valid, topologically coherent workflow definitions; invalid graphs show actionable errors
- [ ] Running a workflow produces a WorkflowRun with proper status transitions and logs; logs are accessible via API and UI
- [ ] Activity Log captures: user actions, workflow runs, integration syncs, and AI actions with correct tenant scoping and filtering
- [ ] Human approval steps can be configured, routed, and auditable; approvals recorded against the relevant WorkflowRun
- [ ] Scheduling, retries, and backoff are implemented with configurable policies; failed runs trigger alerts
- [ ] UI components adhere to the design system with the specified color palette, typography, spacing, and interaction states
- [ ] All array operations are guarded: (items ?? []).map(...) and Array.isArray checks throughout frontend and backend data handling
- [ ] useState initializations for arrays are in place: useState<Workflow[]>([]) and useState<WorkflowRun[]>([])
- [ ] Data models and API contracts validated; responses validated with shape checks

